From: the SK-D840N 7.3 line
Subject: [PATCH] net: zx279133: bring a failed datapath back on the conduit's open

A datapath that cannot be brought back up is marked failed, and the
board has to have a way back from that.  The 6.18 generation had one:
the conduit's open brought the datapath back.  The tree here does not,
and the two paths it does have are both worse than that - the WAN and
the LAN service restore a failed datapath only when a second user is
already there, so a board with one interface up has no way back at all.

The consequence is concrete.  The recovery worker restarts the shared
datapath when a ring is quarantined; if that restart fails, the
datapath is failed and the TX is stopped with it.  The recovery worker
gives up on a failed datapath rather than retrying it, and the XSK
pool's own cycle leaves it failed too.  From there the tree had no
path back.

This patch moves the 6.18 generation's restore into the conduit's open.
DSA opens the conduit when a user port comes up, so "ip link set lan1
down && up" is what brings the board back.

The restore has to come before the hardware check, and that is the
part that is not just a copy.  A datapath that failed leaves the
hardware unprepared - the suspend unprepares it and the resume failure
unprepares it again - so the tree's check would refuse the open before
any restore could run.  A successful restore prepares the hardware
again, so the check stays where it belongs, after the restore.

The restore is a no-op when the datapath is healthy: it returns at
once unless the datapath has failed.  Nothing about a working board
changes.
diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133-netdev.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133-netdev.c
index 3e9f861..037cef6 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133-netdev.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/zte/zx279133-netdev.c
@@ -2138,8 +2138,31 @@ zx279133_lan_netdev_open(struct zx279133_lan_service *service,
 
 	mutex_lock(&eth->datapath_lock);
 	if (!(eth->datapath_users & ZX279133_DATAPATH_USER_LAN) ||
-	    !eth->lan_datapath_ready ||
-	    !READ_ONCE(eth->hardware_prepared)) {
+	    !eth->lan_datapath_ready) {
+		ret = -ENETDOWN;
+		goto out_unlock;
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * The 6.18 generation brought a failed datapath back here, and
+	 * this is the only path left that can.  The WAN and the LAN
+	 * service each restore it only when a second user is already
+	 * there, and a datapath that failed leaves the hardware
+	 * unprepared - so the check below would refuse the open before
+	 * any restore could run.
+	 *
+	 * DSA opens the conduit when a user port comes up, so this is
+	 * where an operator's "ip link set lan1 down && up" brings the
+	 * board back from a datapath that could not be restarted.
+	 *
+	 * The restore is a no-op when the datapath is healthy: it
+	 * returns at once unless the datapath has failed.
+	 */
+	ret = zx279133_datapath_restore_failed_locked(eth);
+	if (ret)
+		goto out_unlock;
+
+	if (!READ_ONCE(eth->hardware_prepared)) {
 		ret = -ENETDOWN;
 		goto out_unlock;
 	}
